Translation patch: `StatusVar::get_value` reads with `ParseBuf`

Change: Each `let mut read = self.value; read.read_uN::<LittleEndian>().map_err(|_| self.value)?` (byteorder on a local `&[u8]`) becomes `ParseBuf(self.value).checked_eat_uN_le()` (or `checked_eat_u8`, `checked_eat(4)`) with a `match` returning `Err(self.value)`; `read.get(..len).ok_or(self.value)?` and `read = &read[len..]` act on `read.0`; the `UpdatedDbNames` loop becomes the recursive helper `updated_db_names` (index loop for `position`).
Why: byteorder's `ReadBytesExt` on `&[u8]` goes through `io::Read`, which Aeneas does not model; closures, `?` on `Option` and early returns in loops: see patch 143.
Behaviour: Identical: on success both read the same little-endian bytes and advance by the same amount; on failure both return `Err(self.value)` immediately (the local reader is then dropped, so how far it advanced is unobservable). The helper pushes the same names (including the upstream behaviour of not skipping the `0` terminator between names).

